 * Attention: Ill-conditioned overlap matrix detected.
 
 * In technical terms, this means that the overlap matrix <\phi_i|\phi_j>
 * (where i and j are basis functions) has eigenvalues that are very close to zero.
 
 * This problem should not normally arise in FHI-aims, but it can, for a very dense
 * basis set. Here is what to do.
 
 * In physical terms, this means that the basis set used in the present calculation is
 * ambiguous: One basis function can be expressed almost exactly as a linear combination
 * of other basis functions. As a result, the representation of Kohn-Sham eigenfunctions
 * in terms of the underlying basis functions is no longer unique.
 * In numerical solvers (especially, an eigenvalue solver, i.e., elementary
 * linear algebra) this ambiguity can lead to badly defined operations, such as divisions
 * by near-zeros and, thus, to unreliable or openly incorrect results.
 * What to do?
 
 * First, check your input geometry. If, for example, you entered half of the intended
 * lattice parameter for a periodic solid by mistake, you would create an extremely
 * dense solid and the basis set would almost certainly be ill-conditioned. Simply
 * correcting the lattice parameter will often fix the issue and allow you to run
 * the intended calculation.
 
 * In general, we have, of course, a way of mitigating the impact of an ill-conditioned
 * basis set. The standard solution is to diagonalize the overlap matrix and use its
 * eigenvectors (which are orthonormal by definition) as a basis set instead, but
 * leave away any of its eigenvectors that have an eigenvalue below a small positive threshold.
 * To do so, use the following keywords in control.in:
 
 * basis_threshold 1d-5
 * override_illconditioning .true.
 
 * However, DO NOT just set these keywords as a standard default. Only do so when needed.
 
 * Why not?
 * In fact, the solution described above works for scenarios in which the overlap matrix
 * is not drastically ill-conditioned, but in extreme scenarios, the numerical impact of
 * an ill-conditioned basis set can lead to inaccurate results even if the above two
 * keywords are set. This is why the code stops at this point - this is an alert
 * to make you aware of the situation. Proceed, but proceed with some caution.
 
 * How can one avoid an ill-conditioned basis set in the first place (if possible)?
 
 * In general, an ill-conditioned overlap matrix (and basis set) arises if the selected
 * basis set is very large. More specifically, the density of basis functions per volume
 * is very large in some area, allowing one to accidentally express one basis function
 * as a linear combination of some others. For example, a molecular basis set could be
 * very large, with atoms close together. In periodic systems, structures which have
 * a very high density of atoms per volume (e.g., crystals involving second-row elements, such
 * as LiF, BeO, AlN etc.) can lead to high densities of basis functions per volume.
 * Basis functions with large radii in periodic systems (e.g., a large cutoff radius, or
 * highly diffuse Gaussian basis functions) can also contribute to ill-conditioning,
 * by creating a very high density of basis functions per volume.
 
 * For DFT, at least, FHI-aims basis sets usually already yield numerically converged results
 * for basis sets that are well below the default threshold for which this warning appears.
 * Thus, a dense solid, in which this warning appears, e.g., when using "tight" settings,
 * may not actually need all the basis functions specified here for adequate convergence.
 * Instead, "intermediate" settings may be a perfectly adequate solution in some scenarios.
 * Furthermore, the standard strategy to remedy the problem by the "basis_threshold" and
 * "override_illconditioning" keywords will usually be problem-free.
 * However, as noted above, you should be aware of the issue, in order to avoid
 * situations in which a drastically ill-conditioned basis set could cause actual problems.
 
 * Lastly, please read the manual entries on the "basis_threshold" and
 * "override_illconditioning" keywords.
 * Apologies for any inconvenience.
 
 *** Ill-conditioned overlap matrix found. Please read the explanation given in the output.
 
